#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198c77be-33a4-5cbd-aa11-6bdd4191dd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374141} {:answer/id #uuid "6556381a-7067-5b49-b676-6d3c5d67e172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374142} {:answer/id #uuid "9e51b078-3e9f-5dea-a9af-d596ee26bc5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374143} {:answer/id #uuid "ddfb13d8-becf-5996-837a-11efb07fcec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374144} {:answer/id #uuid "da79f3cb-6b50-5ceb-bf5c-54aa4f670f09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374145}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374144} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbb48d5b-5877-5dca-aa67-f0774b0d5c49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "954ec2ce-743f-514f-9ac4-50e6ba28f6e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161934" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+2y,\\,v=x-2z,\\,w=4z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le {x}^{2}+2y\\le \\pi ,\\,\\pi \\le x-2z\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+2y,\\,v=x-2z,\\,w=4z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le u\\le \\pi ,\\,\\pi \\le v\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(-1)^{3+3}\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =4(0-2) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{8}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u]_{u=\\pi /2}^{u=\\pi }\\cdot {[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]}_{v=\\pi }^{v=2\\pi }\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(1+0)\\cdot (-1+(-1))\\cdot (4-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b2d1ca3-fd9b-57f2-ac29-4b16c135420c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374154} {:answer/id #uuid "d3fd9c37-4c27-5746-b851-f7b4512dd4ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374155} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c9008f-7ebb-5f0c-9ef4-3aba8c68542d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374156} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d1a911-155e-5064-989c-bdd887a93726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374157} {:answer/id #uuid "730b3a0c-ab79-5dd0-b3b4-eb287d28f6b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374158}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374156} :answer-field/id #uuid "5287f396-f7c5-5f63-8e16-6e1738da83ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b20c6475-5823-5b74-865d-949b70ed5189" :question/math-academy-id "q-162155" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,3\\le y+{x}^{2}\\le 10,\\,1\\le xz\\le 2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,3\\le y+{x}^{2}\\le 10,\\,1\\le xz\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y+{x}^{2},\\,w=xz.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le u\\le 1,\\,3\\le v\\le 10,\\,1\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & x\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =x \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{3}^{10}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}du\\cdot {\\int }_{3}^{10}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{u=1/2}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=3}^{v=10}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)\\cdot (10-3)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74cbe566-120c-5aa2-9d0f-8b0d51b92825" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374161} {:answer/id #uuid "372e9f40-cc59-532c-a782-6948b90925c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374162} {:answer/id #uuid "8a16bd5e-23ba-5f37-8518-594f3a0cc660" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374163} {:answer/id #uuid "455911a2-33b2-56a3-a5e6-863976f27387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374164} {:answer/id #uuid "fe23f6e2-faba-5fcc-a1dc-a65a5472035a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374165}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374163} :answer-field/id #uuid "081961bd-b52b-565f-b46b-523fd43cde20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "2f54bd34-6966-5956-b7d3-23078157be35" :question/math-academy-id "q-162174" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le x+y\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le x+y\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x+y,\\,w=z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,-1\\le w\\le 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2x & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=-1}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1020cd0b-a734-523c-a873-ae4ccc88f60a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374168} {:answer/id #uuid "eded55eb-08dc-5eea-9038-37f16794d4b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374169} {:answer/id #uuid "5a6e2d78-660c-5283-ab55-0c4291532b27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374170} {:answer/id #uuid "9479d209-1358-5b6d-b729-5097d39ca7d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374171} {:answer/id #uuid "db3a4d38-29e5-54a0-b850-59abf8d19cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374172}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374170} :answer-field/id #uuid "11699b7d-5958-5ded-80ec-84552796d392" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374167}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b0695fc2-3499-5ac5-90f7-7e4077884682" :question/math-academy-id "q-162212" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-5\\le x+z\\le 5,\\,0\\le y\\le 10,\\,0\\le y-z\\le 6}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-5\\le x+z\\le 5,,\\,0\\le y\\le 10,\\,0\\le y-z\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+z,\\,v=y,\\,w=y-z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,-5\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 10,\\,0\\le w\\le 6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{10}{\\int }_{0}^{6}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{5}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{10}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{6}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-5}^{u=5}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=10}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=6} \\\\  & =(5-(-5))\\cdot (10-0)\\cdot (6-0) \\\\  & =600.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cedf6411-1c50-521c-9753-b78164606edd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374175} {:answer/id #uuid "2156f8ec-e04e-51b8-bc96-f4f57ab9bd72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374176} {:answer/id #uuid "641c62ce-a323-535d-adec-144d3026c01d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374177} {:answer/id #uuid "08e58611-c6b0-57ca-af8a-daaeefda2147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374178} {:answer/id #uuid "652a7c70-07d1-5457-a185-ceed917c21bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374179}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374176} :answer-field/id #uuid "230795bb-1c46-5274-8505-2f18821e3031" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374174}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "df52d1f2-fb75-5e2b-acc7-74be8b4ade91" :question/math-academy-id "q-162213" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le {x}^{2}-y-z\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{z}\\le 4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le {x}^{2}-y-z\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{z}\\le 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v={x}^{2}-y-z,\\,w=\\sqrt{z}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,1\\le w\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2x & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-2w.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-2w|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}2w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{4}2w\\,dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-2}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [{w}^{2}]_{w=1}^{w=4} \\\\  & =(2-(-2))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (16-1) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375103 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e43e2c3-5048-5e2e-a94a-bcb90d586202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-{u}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375105} {:answer/id #uuid "b13b11c1-2465-561f-a96b-19fa507beb2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-3{v}^{2}+{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375106} {:answer/id #uuid "f152898a-1a3f-57ba-8463-c69b4da0c17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(uv-{v}^{2}+3{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375107} {:answer/id #uuid "3b55205c-68ca-554b-93eb-3fc21cfe0e99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(uv-2{v}^{2}+{v}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375108} {:answer/id #uuid "11837235-5be9-5816-bdf3-58b69896ee4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(uv-5{v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375109}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375106} :answer-field/id #uuid "227ed5b3-0cfb-51b9-820b-b40696387d27" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375104}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db1fee9d-f261-54f6-9563-ad9a8f609d1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38369" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le 2x+y\\le 5,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\n  \n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2x+y$ and $v=y,$ the integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2x+y,\\,v=y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{u-v}{2},\\,y=v.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}(2xy+{x}^{2})\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}[2\\cdot \\frac{u-v}{2}\\cdot v+{\\left(\\frac{u-v}{2}\\right)}^{2}]|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}(uv-{v}^{2}+\\frac{{u}^{2}-2uv+{v}^{2}}{4})\\frac{1}{2}\\,du\\,dv \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{5}(2uv-3{v}^{2}+{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e650b073-7e42-5a29-a5f0-49b40ec48ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}v\\operatorname{ln}⁡u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375125} {:answer/id #uuid "5769b11c-c825-5eeb-8948-b8c15db36333" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}v\\operatorname{ln}⁡u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375126} {:answer/id #uuid "e83ba63d-1a5b-5b95-80a3-c20f0bf16584" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375127} {:answer/id #uuid "2be95ca7-c78a-5b21-980c-e49c00d94791" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375128} {:answer/id #uuid "976dc3c1-b29e-52db-a43c-419b89323c0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3v\\operatorname{ln}⁡u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375129}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375126} :answer-field/id #uuid "e86f996d-8724-5990-98a1-29212aad3144" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "8c4bdde5-7a56-510d-ac15-d76aed6084be" :question/math-academy-id "q-39057" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le 2,\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le 4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=2x+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(2x+z)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=2x+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 6,\\,1\\le w\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$ \n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-(2-0) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(2x+z)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\frac{1}{2}v\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27399e42-4acc-553e-ba4e-5998ae4da3e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,-2)$ and $(0,1)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375337} {:answer/id #uuid "a57c1eee-a86d-5c18-9d82-3d9e99558aee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375338} {:answer/id #uuid "bfb1fb31-2625-5bc1-9263-d7661c3e1d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,1)$ and $(2,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375339} {:answer/id #uuid "ec3841f3-4849-5ca6-b655-2344b4b09e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(-1,-2)$ and $(1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375340} {:answer/id #uuid "30f0e009-0c6d-57a0-b587-090a0c1d88bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$(1,2)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375341} :answer-field/id #uuid "079e80ed-3676-528f-b478-841f2caa3b64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "703e7046-bcfc-54c8-9bb1-c367d4d9ddb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-72427" :question/problem "At which points on the curve ${y}^{2}+2x=2xy+2y-2$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2x & =2xy+2y-2 \\\\ {y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)={y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-2.$  \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y)i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y)j \\\\  & =(2y-2x-2)\\,i-(2-2y)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot i=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ ((2y-2x-2)\\,i-(2-2y)\\,j)\\cdot i & =0 \\\\ 2y-2x-2 & =0 \\\\ 2(y-x-1) & =0 \\\\ y & =x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y=x+1$ into the equation of our curve gives:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+2x-2xy-2y & =-2 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}+2x-2x(x+1)-2(x+1)+2 & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-2x(x+1)-2(x+1)+2(x+1) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-2x(x+1)-\\cancel{2(x+1)}+\\cancel{2(x+1)} & =0 \\\\ (x+1)^{2}-2x(x+1) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(x+1-2x) & =0 \\\\ (x+1)(-x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $x=1$, we get $y=2$ and the corresponding point is $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=-1$, we get $y=0$ and the corresponding point is $(-1,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375495 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0881d141-176a-5207-829e-e7fcf9748ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(v-u)^{4}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375497} {:answer/id #uuid "72802f78-8fa2-5be5-8cb9-94054dadb960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375498} {:answer/id #uuid "9d50078b-ccc0-5312-be32-aa00de941511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(v-u)^{2}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375499} {:answer/id #uuid "ce22a068-82fa-5eda-a3a4-b436e880bc4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375500} {:answer/id #uuid "f22feb1c-862a-595f-aaba-980169e06dd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(v-u)^{2}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375501}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375498} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b052ab4-b874-5259-8af3-5e39e362471e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375496}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ff0203f2-b0b4-5368-8eaf-f5cdf929fd73" :question/math-academy-id "q-82808" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le y-x\\le 1,\\,0\\le x+y\\le 1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=y-x$ and $v=x+y,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}8xy\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=y-x,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=y-x,\\,v=x+y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{v-u}{2},\\,y=\\frac{v+u}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}8xy\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}8\\left(\\frac{v-u}{2}\\right)\\left(\\frac{v+u}{2}\\right)|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}2({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}({v}^{2}-{u}^{2})\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "661f11a2-58e2-5323-aed0-4c84d298b7a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{3u-2v}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375504} {:answer/id #uuid "ac200449-dc33-5fa1-b080-2757eb77e01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2u-3v}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375505} {:answer/id #uuid "32bc657c-6f00-59b6-8ae4-b48679d4067b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2u-v}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375506} {:answer/id #uuid "abd6ec5f-e42a-51e9-a175-fd591e2adebf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{u+v}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375507} {:answer/id #uuid "624cfbb7-2e59-5fce-9fc2-e4f5ba306b77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{3u-2v}\\,du\\,dv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375508}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375505} :answer-field/id #uuid "50dace88-55f0-5aaf-a1fc-7d3cc1e32c2f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d437ff63-9f7b-5463-a5bf-dc3f2e150b31" :question/math-academy-id "q-82827" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le 2x+5y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x+3y\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=2x+5y$ and $v=x+3y,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+5y,\\,v=x+3y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }3\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nIn this case, we first need to solve the transformation equations\n\n$$\nu=2x+5y,\\,v=x+3y\n$$\n\n \nfor $x$ and $y$, which gives\n\n$$\nx=3u-5v,\\,y=2v-u.\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, performing the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{e}^{x+y}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{3u-5v+2v-u}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{2u-3v}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}{e}^{2u-3v}\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{e}^{2u-3v}\\,du\\,dv.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375509 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b519e50-b326-56a3-8360-01cf01bef0a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375511} {:answer/id #uuid "8717345d-af4e-5f8b-876a-46ab0b5392c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(u+v)^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375512} {:answer/id #uuid "de05559f-528d-50d8-9fa6-bf9cc362a558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}(u+v)^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375513} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4bc111-f102-5feb-91f1-f0edd3cc5425" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375514} {:answer/id #uuid "8ecb057f-1869-565e-8e7e-c9b2d50df235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375515}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375514} :answer-field/id #uuid "da395623-3256-578e-8977-74807708542d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375510}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cab9f14a-bb15-51f5-bbda-2778b7330c33" :question/math-academy-id "q-83647" :question/problem "Consider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x+y\\le 3,\\,-4\\le y-x\\le -1\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables $u=x+y$ and $v=y-x,$ the integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}3(x+y)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\ncan be written in terms of $u$ and $v$ as" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dx\\,dy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|du\\,dv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & =\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-4\\le v\\le -1\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3(x+y)^{2}\\,dx\\,dy & ={∬}_{Δ}3{u}^{2}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}3{u}^{2}|\\frac{1}{2}|\\text{d}u\\,\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{3}{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}{u}^{2}\\,dv\\,du.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44e18b50-5a34-5995-980b-2b315b3a6f0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375518} {:answer/id #uuid "594f9a4a-d6e9-5df8-a308-2eb4cbda2ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375519} {:answer/id #uuid "01cb4d30-859b-537e-bd09-5b716f143dff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375520} {:answer/id #uuid "047a39d6-26a5-5925-9b60-74ca9ec1ef3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375521} {:answer/id #uuid "88731088-8329-5a12-8959-6718187bcd65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375522}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375521} :answer-field/id #uuid "83dc21d0-96ad-5f47-bb56-3352df831f55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375517}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "03366686-a1a7-5aa1-bce5-fc561e685ebd" :question/math-academy-id "q-83651" :question/problem "Use a change of variables to evaluate the double integral \n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,dxdy,\n$$\n\nwhere $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,|x+y|\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dxdy={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v),y(u,v))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1**: Find a suitable change of variables.\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $D,$\n\n$$\n1\\le x\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le x+y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x\\,\\text{and}\\,v=x+y.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uv$-plane is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & \\text{ }\\text{ }0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 5**: Perform the change of variables, and evaluate the integral:\n\nPerforming the change of variables, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,|1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v[{\\int }_{1}^{3}u\\,du]dv \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}dv \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv \\\\  & =4[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a066e71c-60d5-5fbb-80e0-33c201104192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the listed" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375806} {:answer/id #uuid "251b33e0-026f-5d55-b464-0243c1951e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "K(x,y,z)=i-j-k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375807} {:answer/id #uuid "f666e84d-6691-5a63-8975-137451851b83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "F(x,y,z)=y\\,i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375808} {:answer/id #uuid "e4e84d30-dd2a-5fa6-ae2d-25fd3bdb8810" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "G(x,y,z)=i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375809} {:answer/id #uuid "11bfffa1-bd3e-551d-accb-96f12bb32b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "H(x,y,z)=-i" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375810}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375810} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ad7530e-1d52-5779-9ca6-d5f03c6b618e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375805}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "76f85577-2f56-52c7-abc0-220aedba2d13" :question/math-academy-id "q-122667" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9db14c13d03cb99846a40622a9633f229e8bd18942f2d03635961529e6970771.png)\n\nWhich of the following could be the function that generates the vector field above?" :question/worked-solution "We notice the following:\n\n- Each vector in our vector field is parallel to the $x$-axis. So, the $j$ and $k$ components of our vector field are zero everywhere. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nK(x,y,z)=i-j-k\n$$\n\n- Moreover, each vector in our vector field is pointing in the *negative* $x$-direction, so the $i$ component of our vector field is negative. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nG(x,y,z)=i\n$$\n\n- Finally, we note that each vector in our vector field has the same length, so the $i$ component of our vector field is constant. Therefore, we can rule out the following option:\n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=y\\,i\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the diagram corresponds to the vector field $H(x,y,z)=-i.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376124 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b9f18b-ec2f-51d5-ae14-92461f9907ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $wv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376126} {:answer/id #uuid "5bacd98b-62c2-5e98-a210-80793aec69cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376127} {:answer/id #uuid "13bfe028-f756-50dc-a99d-e4bd7e879772" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the parabolic cylinder $w=2{v}^{2}.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376128} {:answer/id #uuid "8ad07193-94a4-5bd2-b02f-54c7365c5656" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the plane $w=-2v.$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376129} {:answer/id #uuid "69b5ad50-abba-5674-a016-be9139fe02be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The critical points lie on the $uv-$plane." :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376130}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376128} :answer-field/id #uuid "88cb516a-a7eb-5761-97af-05385415c8d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186376125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a710b40d-30b7-5142-a877-a2aee690f184" :question/math-academy-id "q-161813" :question/problem "Find the locations of all of the critical points of the transformation $T,$ where $T$ is defined as\n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{u} \\\\ {v}^{2}+w \\\\ vw\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "For a transformation \n\n$$\nT:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\nthe Jacobian determinant of $T,$ denoted $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)},$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} & \\frac{∂x}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v} & \\frac{∂y}{∂w} \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & \\frac{∂z}{∂v} & \\frac{∂z}{∂w}\\end{vmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, we have \n\n$$\nx(u,v,w)=\\frac{1}{u},\\,y(u,v,w)={v}^{2}+w,\\,z(u,v,w)=vw.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂v} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂x}{∂w} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v} & =2v & \\,\\frac{∂y}{∂w} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂u} & =0 & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v} & =w & \\,\\frac{∂z}{∂w} & =v\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding Jacobian determinant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2v & 1 \\\\ 0 & w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\begin{vmatrix}2v & 1 \\\\ w & v\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}(2{v}^{2}-w).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe function has critical points when $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}=0.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)} & =0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}(2{v}^{2}-w) & =0 \\\\ w-2{v}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation $w-2{v}^{2}=0$ defines the parabolic cylinder $w=2{v}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377695 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d118fc5-1c52-5566-be98-a9707e774937" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377697} {:answer/id #uuid "30aa323e-111a-55db-ae16-f96b2c70da3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377698} {:answer/id #uuid "563f0bf5-f3be-54dc-b44e-fce9a03892d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377699} {:answer/id #uuid "a81b9d36-0a30-5151-bf36-ec51a5b12490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377700} {:answer/id #uuid "f84b3786-3b86-57a7-acce-0bc703bbfb0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377701}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377699} :answer-field/id #uuid "117ca5de-bfde-5504-8e9b-162dc1ee9296" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d22711b-d1d6-5f06-ab4a-74275f71d77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-74920" :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1}=\\frac{1\\cdot (-3)-(-3)}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{xy-y}{x-1} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}\\frac{(x-1)y}{x-1} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,-3)}y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377708 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8958422f-4c02-53af-a3e5-28f8445d5bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377710} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7dc054-8a42-5213-b120-534bafd7ab86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377711} {:answer/id #uuid "7ad9cc3c-2948-54af-96ab-97ac3dc5dd75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377712} {:answer/id #uuid "de9768d4-de86-55ac-a72b-b0470b48216c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377713} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2466e9-2081-57a2-b9cc-0260610356d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377714}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377713} :answer-field/id #uuid "f14349ec-0b87-5b7c-b87c-2368f3340f77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186377709}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "6323a167-9fc1-5848-81f1-63d34a9c63ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-74965" :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\frac{{0}^{2}\\cdot 0}{{0}^{3}+{0}^{3}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths. \n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the line $y=2x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=2x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}+8{x}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}+{y}^{3}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da8b2db2-4123-5665-b738-98ed34b75b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378624} {:answer/id #uuid "1d3687c4-99c4-5acf-aa17-8ee4a667504b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378625} {:answer/id #uuid "5cfdc49f-f710-579f-a121-34bd82cc68c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378626} {:answer/id #uuid "5bef6f1c-9c50-5077-83d6-7937a6ae0e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378627} {:answer/id #uuid "6b4dca40-4471-5b61-bf8b-e5086c017493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378628}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378628} :answer-field/id #uuid "b89502e5-3b74-56d2-882e-fcb6a0ee1e92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378623}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "88dbd745-2040-5337-99bf-90ad013cc0a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-129327" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}y.$" :question/worked-solution "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous for all values of $x$ and $y.$ So, we can calculate the limit by substituting $(2,\\pi )$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,\\pi )}x\\operatorname{cos}y & =2\\operatorname{cos}\\pi  \\\\  & =2(-1) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378629 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54bad6f7-afaf-5211-a170-c6ab451f5c01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378631} {:answer/id #uuid "b3eb5e8a-c30b-5e5d-b3dd-5e84a503fb93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378632} {:answer/id #uuid "9a772183-f18a-5957-a18c-b8eaf33e3416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378633} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d48428-fe8b-54bd-8382-9007264d0a0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378634} {:answer/id #uuid "816585ea-530d-530b-acda-63e8b7815463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378635}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378632} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2035409-00f4-5463-8bd1-cb9d0396a3d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378630}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5bb79452-99aa-5770-9532-a1808e3b1fe8" :question/math-academy-id "q-129334" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\frac{0}{{0}^{2}-0(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}-xy} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{x}{x(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{x}}{\\enclose{updiagonalstrike}{x}(x-y)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{1}{x-y} & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{0+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378636 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed9a8298-54cd-5904-87cc-0773ceed9484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378638} {:answer/id #uuid "16292b9f-eddf-569d-8d1e-2dd760b68415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378639} {:answer/id #uuid "40076bfe-6573-5b16-91f1-cdb51a556125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378640} {:answer/id #uuid "47ea9746-2601-59cf-97ee-1ee69879d0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378641} {:answer/id #uuid "6cca0148-f328-5676-8042-40e9ef488295" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378642}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378640} :answer-field/id #uuid "f471c6a4-8548-5953-bc64-722a32227684" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186378637}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "a45c5a39-df0e-5ca7-830f-279fde3ab785" :question/math-academy-id "q-129403" :question/problem "Determine $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{0+0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{0}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{0}{2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379335 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44db07ec-2191-5d66-816a-ba7bc6e87607" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379337} {:answer/id #uuid "70e9e99a-aa36-5b43-acc5-2856b465bc6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379338} {:answer/id #uuid "6b44e8c6-a9c5-5929-9a59-23732907aed8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379339} {:answer/id #uuid "966effd4-44b8-544e-806d-5177e26f2538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379340} {:answer/id #uuid "61ee2821-c169-5d9c-8600-b826ca6f7e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379341}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379339} :answer-field/id #uuid "81a14916-58ff-5162-b12f-6b1417129ae2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379336}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bdcec5e6-2531-5f95-ae29-3c0f54fe0692" :question/math-academy-id "q-19135" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,a\\,|=8$ and the angle between vectors $a$ and $b$ is $\\theta ={120}^{∘}.$ Find the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{b}\\,a & =|\\,a\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9679c15f-eb66-5f8f-bc63-1aefe179a953" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379344} {:answer/id #uuid "0020d0a8-0606-5b18-a7a8-958973e37be8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379345} {:answer/id #uuid "c47bb5b3-5921-5681-9fee-e5068d117e86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379346} {:answer/id #uuid "8be61420-e5d2-50d1-9814-aa6788849373" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379347} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f0dff6-0e8a-5003-9ccb-b4ae6d63b705" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379348}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379348} :answer-field/id #uuid "67ba134b-4d9f-5cf6-b38a-1dcb5e8de13b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6f76a4d7-83bc-53e9-bba0-121ef750dc15" :question/math-academy-id "q-237019" :question/problem "Let $p=i+2j+xk$ and $q=3i+6j-3k.$ Given that the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q$ is zero, find the value of $x.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $p$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $q$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\np⊥q\\,⟹\\,p\\cdot q=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & ={p}_{x}\\cdot {q}_{x}+{p}_{y}\\cdot {q}_{y}+{p}_{z}\\cdot {q}_{z} \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 6+x\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =15-3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p\\cdot q & =0 \\\\ 15-3x & =0 \\\\ 3x & =15 \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379360 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58363d5c-920b-5c26-a09f-feadb48d18af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379362}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379362} :answer-field/id #uuid "569ff198-8871-5c1e-bb50-e29a59fe2e29" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "59d8a9a7-8383-53f6-9c1f-7b708ece3184" :question/math-academy-id "q-318969" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,m\\,|=12$ and the angle between vectors $m$ and $n$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Find the scalar projection of $m$ onto $n.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,m & =|\\,m\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12cos\\,\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379363 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00efebbd-b34e-5e1d-90ba-a1a1a499e767" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379365}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379365} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e556b82-e5e4-5c2f-9171-f94a4a3a1d96" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2aae90e0-11e3-5ee4-8f7d-d33e1592b8e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-318978" :question/problem "Let $r=⟨-3,2,4⟩$ and $s=⟨1,2,-1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $r$ onto $s.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{s}\\,r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{s}\\,r & =\\frac{r\\cdot s}{|\\,s\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{r}_{x}{s}_{x}+{r}_{y}{s}_{y}+{r}_{z}{s}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{s}_{x}^{2}+{s}_{y}^{2}+{s}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-3)(1)+(2)(2)+(4)(-1)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3+4-4}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379366 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a08d5080-b0cc-529b-927c-57d7b8fb919c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379368} :answer-field/id #uuid "aedce65b-2d90-57c8-9df1-ce436a0e55d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "64a835fe-a6b3-525d-8366-194469689a7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-318984" :question/problem "Let $u=⟨3,1,-2⟩$ and $v=⟨1,1,1⟩.$ Find the scalar projection of $u$ onto $v.$\n\n${\\text{comp}}_{v}\\,u=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the scalar projection formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{v}\\,u & =\\frac{u\\cdot v}{|\\,v\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{{u}_{x}{v}_{x}+{u}_{y}{v}_{y}+{u}_{z}{v}_{z}}{\\sqrt{{v}_{x}^{2}+{v}_{y}^{2}+{v}_{z}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3)(1)+(1)(1)+(-2)(1)}{\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3+1-2}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379369 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eb69b0f2-a946-5ec4-8b5a-8dc7a021c0c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8/3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379371}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379371} :answer-field/id #uuid "681c19f4-13bc-5301-abe1-89b3c65ef8d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cf60000a-0fab-5712-856b-db3cc64a89f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-319257" :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ x\\end{bmatrix}.$ Given that the scalar projection of $a$ onto $b$ is $0,$ find the value of $x.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ is a nonzero vector, its scalar projection onto another nonzero vector $b$ equals zero only when the vectors are perpendicular. \n\nSo, we must have\n\n$$\na⊥b\\,⟹\\,a\\cdot b=0.\n$$\n\nComputing the dot product, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{x}{b}_{x}+{a}_{y}{b}_{y}+{a}_{z}{b}_{z} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot x \\\\  & =3x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the dot product equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+8 & =0 \\\\ 3x & =-8 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379931 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1c473f5-dd16-51e8-972f-8503bdea49f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379933} {:answer/id #uuid "2779943f-aae7-50af-b0c6-2a0c3b6a854a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379934} {:answer/id #uuid "b957948e-1fb9-5dc4-95a5-6c0f15c55de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379935} {:answer/id #uuid "8d86eb8a-95c5-5e77-ae3c-4d5771dc8a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379936} {:answer/id #uuid "91314d14-31ca-547e-a15e-ba542c305c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379937}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379936} :answer-field/id #uuid "adbe73b9-93eb-5eb7-bc8e-c67c1d6a7d03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379932}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6de53f7c-a016-5a05-a942-7eae5f3fae21" :question/math-academy-id "q-71528" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,c\\,|=10$ and the angle between vectors $c$ and $d$ is $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Find the scalar projection of $c$ onto $d.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{d}\\,c & =|\\,c\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ae36112-5d67-541f-8618-63c91ad25131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379940} {:answer/id #uuid "e10ff8bd-44a5-5190-acb6-446d018c9704" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379941} {:answer/id #uuid "4323e039-1d0d-5d26-a6ff-3ece5246be8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379942} {:answer/id #uuid "aa3e86b8-2db7-52af-8116-994cefeff282" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379943} {:answer/id #uuid "8dbbcd7c-a0a4-54df-8f2f-41d5c56772be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379944}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379943} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d208d89-fa96-5758-89de-b77ebf18b4c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379939}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "90337284-e168-5a3e-b426-ab4b7cb72992" :question/math-academy-id "q-71533" :question/problem "Suppose $|\\,p\\,|=2$ and the angle between vectors $p$ and $q$ is $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.$ Find the scalar projection of $p$ onto $q.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula for the scalar projection, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{comp}}_{q}\\,p & =|\\,p\\,|\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380041 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56a685e0-c5f1-5af2-896d-00b1934e31d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380043} {:answer/id #uuid "24ca23db-bb35-57cc-a828-65a684d47d57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380044} {:answer/id #uuid "395c8b3a-034c-5c76-8114-a06f611b180e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380045} {:answer/id #uuid "19c8cd1c-6dc3-50a3-9875-7799ef849dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380046} {:answer/id #uuid "8088eaef-6ff9-5ae3-a905-dce7305a0cf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380047}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380045} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b999621-685d-57ae-875e-b93bc57b0422" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "6df7cb51-78c3-5b67-a139-cdde39ca3596" :question/math-academy-id "q-74910" :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z}=\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\sqrt{x-y+z}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the expression under the radical is negative. However, the point $(3,1,-1)$ does not cause the  expression under the radical  to be negative, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-3,1,-1)$ into the function: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (3,1,-1)}\\sqrt{x-y+z} & =\\sqrt{3-1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3-1-1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380048 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d0157d0-7447-5804-b79a-d9a9496f35c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380050} {:answer/id #uuid "44b22efb-654b-53b0-b5bd-3dbebc607aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380051} {:answer/id #uuid "0767a721-3b28-5414-a3e4-a98c298c7320" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380052} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf8c18e-328f-5ffc-8a5c-aec2375f7d52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380053} {:answer/id #uuid "d27bd26c-9c31-59e2-8622-050a5d783466" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380054}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380053} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fd8a32c-5a54-5319-9722-fa439ed2e8e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "70818c9b-be75-5377-b1b9-218a9a896c5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-74913" :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,-2)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}=$" :question/worked-solution "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y,z)=\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(1,1,-2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(1,1,-2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (1,1,-2)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}z}{{x}^{2}+xy+xz-yz} & =\\frac{2\\cdot {1}^{2}+{1}^{2}\\cdot (-2)}{{1}^{2}+1\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2)-1\\cdot (-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2-2}{1+1-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380055 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "392ee925-0a86-58e9-be9d-088d11882899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380057} {:answer/id #uuid "95b9f301-3e4c-5b4a-8f90-c92aeb09e382" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380058} {:answer/id #uuid "988389d2-83d7-50e4-b103-318efb7628de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380059} {:answer/id #uuid "2f0a15c7-e641-5ba1-805d-360eea7a6554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380060} {:answer/id #uuid "b8655f26-6734-5a2e-bdb9-ca9cf96b1f56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380061}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186380060} :answer-field/id #uuid "b9e2ee50-e13c-591e-b10c-41485a4529eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186380056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "142437af-f631-571c-967f-34ef02138536" :question/math-academy-id "q-74919" :question/problem "Find  $\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x}=\\frac{{0}^{2}-0\\cdot 2}{{0}^{3}-2\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-xy}{{x}^{3}+2x} & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x(x-y)}{x({x}^{2}+2)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it using direct substitution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,2)}\\frac{x-y}{{x}^{2}+2} & =\\frac{0-2}{0+2} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $-1.$"}]}
